# Controller Conventions

## DTOs

- Always use DTO classes for request bodies, query params, and responses — never use plain objects or inline types.
- Place DTOs in a file named `<name>.dto.ts` alongside the controller (e.g. `health.dto.ts`).
- Decorate every DTO property with `@ApiProperty()` from `@nestjs/swagger`.
- Always include both `description` and `example` in every `@ApiProperty()`. The description should explain the meaning of the field in plain English, not just restate its name.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — no description
@ApiProperty({ example: '2026-04-12T00:00:00.000Z' })
time: string;

// ✅ GOOD
@ApiProperty({ description: 'Current server time', example: '2026-04-12T00:00:00.000Z' })
time: string;
```

## Controllers

- Annotate every controller with `@ApiTags()`.
- Annotate every endpoint with the appropriate `@ApiOperation()` and `@ApiResponse()` decorators.
- Never put business logic inside a controller method. Delegate all logic to an injected service.

## Tests

Every controller requires two test files. Create them alongside the implementation:

- `src/modules/<feature>/<feature>.controller.spec.ts` — unit tests; mock the service with `jest.fn()`.
- `test/<feature>/<feature>.e2e-spec.ts` — e2e tests; wire up a real `NestApplication` with `ValidationPipe` and mock only the TypeORM repository via `getRepositoryToken`.

Cover every endpoint in both files: happy path, 404 for unknown ids, and 400 for invalid request bodies.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — business logic in controller
@Get()
check(): HealthResponseDto {
  return { status: 'ok', version, time: new Date().toISOString() };
}

// ✅ GOOD — logic lives in the service
@Get()
@ApiOperation({ summary: 'Check service health' })
@ApiResponse({ status: 200, type: HealthResponseDto })
check(): HealthResponseDto {
  return this.healthService.check();
}
```
